{"id": "live-369", "title": "Phoenix Must Defend Data-Center Zoning Rules or Face $54M Takings Claim, Arizona Court Rules", "category": "zoning-land-use", "status": "ruling", "date": "2026-10-28", "jurisdiction": "Maricopa County Superior Court, Arizona", "state": "AZ", "amount": "$54 million in lost property value claimed", "source": "live", "source_url": "https://www.kjzz.org/politics/2026-10-28/court-phoenix-must-prove-data-center-rules-are-a-necessary-protection-or-face-54m-lawsuit", "summary": "A Maricopa County Superior Court judge ruled that the City of Phoenix must prove at a full evidentiary hearing that its 2025 data-center zoning ordinance is necessary to protect public health, safety, or welfare, or face liability under Arizona's Proposition 207 to developer West Buckeye Road L.P. (WBR). The court dismissed WBR's separate claim that the ordinance's own text created a right to sue for compensation, but let the Proposition 207 claim proceed, with an evidentiary hearing set for November 17, 2026.", "significance": "The ruling makes Phoenix's permitting regime for data centers a test case for Arizona's Proposition 207, which lets landowners recover the drop in fair market value caused by a land-use law unless the government proves a narrow statutory exemption applies. More than a dozen other landowners have filed similar claims against the same ordinance, so the outcome of WBR's November hearing is likely to shape settlement leverage and litigation strategy for that entire docket, and for any other Prop 207 jurisdiction regulating data centers.", "judge": null, "tags": ["zoning", "data-center", "proposition-207", "takings", "arizona"]}
